BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 677.0 Tm (Files use directory entries with a first-cluster reference and logical byte length. The) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 660.0 Tm (allocator links each cluster to a successor in a FAT, so sequential file access follows the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 643.0 Tm (chain. One table can be copied to a second FAT, but the copies need not always be in sync) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 626.0 Tm (after partial writes or faults. Allocation is a graph traversal with consistency checks,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 609.0 Tm (not a free-space bitmap scan.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 580.0 Tm (The FAT16 root directory has a bounded number of fixed 32-byte entries. Subdirectories are) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 563.0 Tm (ordinary cluster chains and can grow if free clusters exist. Names were originally DOS 8.3,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 546.0 Tm (with long names encoded through ordered and checksummed VFAT directory entries. Cluster) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 529.0 Tm (size affects addressable volume size as well as wasted tail space in small files.) Tj ET
